    ## SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tbody>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning to Balance</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How to Balance Social and School Activities</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe the process of adapting to a new environment. Interactive: Facilitate peer feedback sessions on presentations.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe the process of balancing school and social activities, explaining strategies to manage time and reduce stress (e.g., "I balance my schoolwork and social life by planning my study time around social events and using a calendar to stay organized. This helps me avoid feeling overwhelmed.")<br>Can analyze persuasive pieces about managing relaxation effectively and provide constructive feedback during peer sessions (e.g., "The article argues that students who set clear boundaries between school and personal time tend to have lower stress levels.")<br>Can discuss the benefits of balancing school work and relaxation (e.g., "The story suggests that having time for both schoolwork and relaxation helps students stay focused.")</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    ## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #e0e0e0;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying three images on the board: one showing a student studying intensely, another showing friends laughing at a social event, and a third showing someone practicing a hobby like drawing or playing an instrument. Students are asked to briefly discuss with a partner which image best represents their current situation and why. The teacher asks, "What challenges have you been facing to balance these activities?" encouraging use of the present perfect continuous and vocabulary such as 'workload' and 'social life'.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models target grammar and pronunciation. For example, "I have been feeling overwhelmed because of my school activities lately, but I'm trying to make time for relaxation." The teacher then highlights the short /ɪ/ sound in 'activities' and the long /iː/ sound in 'relieve' (e.g., "Balancing school activities can help relieve stress."). The teacher also models giving suggestions: "You might try to schedule your downtime." and expressing opinions: "I think setting boundaries is essential for wellbeing."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I have been studying for my exams,' does that mean I started studying just now, or some time ago and I'm still doing it?" (Expected answer: Some time ago and still ongoing.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'You might try mindfulness,' am I telling you what to do, or giving a possible suggestion?" (Expected answer: Giving a possible suggestion.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning to Balance")</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue (approx. 1-2 minutes) between two high school students, Alex and Sofia, discussing their ongoing efforts to manage school, hobbies, and social life. The dialogue will naturally incorporate the present perfect continuous, second conditional, and modals for possibility. It will feature vocabulary like 'routine', 'prioritize', 'downtime', 'procrastination', 'flexible', 'stress', 'wellbeing', and highlight the pronunciation of /ɪ/ and /iː/ (e.g., "It's difficult with all these *activities*," "I wish I could *relieve* some pressure.").</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interacting orally to exchange information, express opinions, and give suggestions about managing personal time and responsibilities effectively.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the dialogue twice. First, for gist: "What is the main topic Alex and Sofia are discussing?" Then, for specific information:</p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: "Alex has been trying to manage his time better by reducing his social activities."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Information Matching: Match each student (Alex/Sofia) with a specific strategy they mention (e.g., using a calendar, joining a sports club, practicing mindfulness).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Open-ended: "If Sofia had more free time, what activity would she do to unwind?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking accurately using Present Perfect Continuous, Second Conditional, and Modals for possibility (should, might, can, could, must, would, may). Incorporating vocabulary: 'balance', 'routine', 'schedule', 'stress', 'relaxation', 'mindfulness', 'prioritize', 'manage', 'organize', 'relieve'. Practicing /ɪ/ and /iː/ sounds.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Sentence Completion (Speaking):</b> Students are given sentence stems related to balancing activities and complete them orally using the target grammar and vocabulary.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I have been __________ (verb) to __________ (verb) my schoolwork and social life." (e.g., *I have been planning my schedule to manage my schoolwork and social life.*)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I had more __________ (noun) after school, I __________ (modal) __________ (verb) __________ (activity) to relax." (e.g., *If I had more flexibility after school, I would join the art club to relax.*)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You __________ (modal) __________ (verb) __________ (strategy) to __________ (verb) stress." (e.g., *You might try mindfulness to relieve stress.*)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled Pair Substitution Drill:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides a model sentence, and students take turns substituting underlined words with new vocabulary words from a provided list or their own ideas, maintaining the grammatical structure.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Model: "If I had more <u>downtime</u>, I <u>would feel</u> less <u>overwhelmed</u>."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 1: "If I had more <u>flexibility</u>, I <u>would have</u> more <u>wellbeing</u>."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 2: "If I had more <u>time management</u>, I <u>could prioritize</u> my <u>activities</u> better." (Teacher explicitly reminds students to articulate /ɪ/ in 'activities'.)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Peer Question & Answer:</b> Students interview each other using a set of guided questions that require the target grammar and vocabulary. They focus on pronunciation, especially /ɪ/ and /iː/.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What hobbies have you been practicing to unwind from your busy routine?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you could set new boundaries for your social life and schoolwork, what would they be?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific strategies might help students relieve their stress effectively?"</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning to Balance")</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Peer Consulting Session: Balancing Act Solutions (Small Group Collaboration & Presentation)</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in groups of three. Each student will, in turn, describe a personal challenge they have been facing in balancing their school and social activities, incorporating the Present Perfect Continuous and specific vocabulary (e.g., "I have been struggling with procrastination and it's affecting my wellbeing."). The other two group members will listen actively and then provide constructive suggestions for improvement using Modals for possibility (e.g., "You might want to allocate specific time for downtime," "You should prioritize your tasks," "If you organized your schedule, you would feel less stress.") and express their opinions on the benefits of these strategies (e.g., "I think effective time management can truly relieve a lot of pressure."). The groups will be encouraged to discuss diverse cultural perspectives on relaxation and time management. Students should focus on clear articulation, especially distinguishing between /ɪ/ in 'activities' and /iː/ in 'relieve'. After each round, students switch roles so everyone gets a chance to present a challenge and offer solutions.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist & Peer Assessment</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates and uses an observation checklist to note students' use of target grammar, vocabulary, pronunciation, and pragmatic functions during the "Peer Consulting Session." Students also complete a short peer assessment form for their group members.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate actively in the "Peer Consulting Session," describing challenges, giving suggestions, and expressing opinions. Each student should aim to speak for at least 1-2 minutes about their 'balancing act' and provide at least two distinct suggestions to their peers.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation, specifically distinguishing /ɪ/ in 'activities' and /iː/ in 'relieve'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Appropriate and varied use of nouns, verbs, and adjectives related to the theme (e.g., 'mindfulness', 'prioritize', 'overwhelming').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> Correct application of Present Perfect Continuous, Second Conditional, and Modals for possibility.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Ideas are presented logically and are easy to follow.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and interaction:</b> Actively listening, providing relevant feedback and suggestions, encouraging inclusive communication.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new strategy for managing balance that I learned today, and how might I apply it?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did I use modals to give suggestions or express possibilities during the activity?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's speaking activities did I find most challenging, and why?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one specific improvement I can make in my speaking to better articulate my ideas or use new grammar/vocabulary?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The speaking activities, especially the 'Peer Consulting Session,' worked well to encourage interactive communication and the application of target grammar and vocabulary. Students demonstrated good understanding of the concepts of 'balance' and 'time management'. Some students still hesitated with the second conditional, needing more practice with hypothetical statements. The pronunciation focus on /ɪ/ and /iː/ was successful for most, but a few students require further reinforcement. For the next lesson, I will provide sentence starters for writing tasks that build directly on the suggestions and opinions shared today, ensuring clear connection and scaffolding for written expression. I will also incorporate a quick review of the second conditional.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The strategies and opinions discussed in today's speaking lesson will serve as foundational content for Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), where students will write persuasive paragraphs or short essays about effective ways to balance school and social activities.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>